Brig can store activation keys in PostgreSQL (`activation_keys` table), selected by `postgresMigration.activationKeys` (`cassandra`, `migration-to-postgresql` or `postgresql`). There is no data migration: `migration-to-postgresql` behaves like `cassandra`, and keys issued before switching to `postgresql` are lost (users have to request a new activation code). With PostgreSQL, checking an activation code and consuming a retry happen in a single statement, so concurrent attempts cannot exceed the retry limit. Expired keys are removed every 5 minutes by the `expire-activation-keys` pg_cron job when pg_cron is available.
